var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler","page":"Beneath compile","text":"","title":"Tempest.Compiler"},{"category":"section","location":"compiler/#Beneath-compile","page":"Beneath compile","text":"","title":"Beneath compile"},{"category":"section","location":"compiler/#Plans","page":"Beneath compile","text":"","title":"Plans"},{"category":"module","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler","page":"Beneath compile","text":"How a program becomes kernels: the program, index and plan levels, the cover, code generation, the kernel cache, and prepared programs. Its public names are what packages outside it need: beneath compile, a program traced (trace) and prepared (prepare, bind!, execute!, capture!, replay! and outputs), for a step run in place; the language programs are written in, which Tempest makes its own, and what an extension writes traced methods with (TracedArray, Traced, contract); what implementations read of a plan beyond IR, and what they build kernels with (the kernel cache, generated and concurrently); and the protocol a package that adds a target defines (see Target).\n\nIts submodules are IR, the vocabulary its levels share and implementations read, and Implement, the interface kernel packages implement. Their public names are what kernel packages read; the compiler also uses what they keep to themselves, such as the arithmetic that builds index expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.trace","page":"Beneath compile","text":"trace(f, args...) -> Program\n\nRun f on stand-ins for args and record what it computes. Arrays among args, also inside tuples, named tuples, structs, mutable ones too, and vectors of other things than numbers, become the program's inputs. Numbers are constants, as configuration is: an integer can be a size, a layer's epsilon or a rule's rate is a number of the program, and a number that varies between executions is an array, [x] say. Each struct and vector is built anew around the stand-ins, so f never writes the caller's, and a vector's length is the program's. The results are arrays and numbers in the same kinds of structure, a vector among them built as map builds one, its element type the join of its elements'; any other leaf, nothing or a layer's function, is returned as it is.\n\nf itself is code here: the arrays it holds, a closure's captures or a callable struct's fields, are constants of the program, copied when traced. To make them inputs, pass f among the arguments, as compile does: trace((f, x) -> f(x), f, x).\n\nf may write into an input, as a layer updates its running statistics: what it wrote is the input's contents when the program ends, a result after those f returns, which replaces the input (see Program). Executed, the program leaves it in the caller's array, as f would.\n\njulia> p = trace(x -> sum(abs2, x), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\nProgram with 3 nodes: Float64(3,) → Float64\n  %1 = argument :: Float64(3,)\n  %2 = map abs2 %1 :: Float64(3,)\n  %3 = reduce sum over (1,) %2 :: Float64  [result]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.trace"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.prepare","page":"Beneath compile","text":"prepare(program, inputs...; target, measure = :none, replaces = (), by_reference = (), point = false, recompute = (), ride = (), partition = true, build = true, verify) -> Executable\n\nPlan program for the shapes and types of inputs and build its kernels on target, chosen from the inputs' array types by default.\n\nmeasure: which claims are timed, where the target can, and kept in the tuning record: :none, the default, none, though the times in the record are still used; :close, the options whose estimates are within the close-call factor of the best; :all, every option of every group and both sides of every comparison. true and false mean :close and :none, and the environment's TEMPEST_MEASURE overrides the default.\nreplaces: state, as pairs result => argument of positions among the program's results and its arguments, alike in structure, shapes and types: after each execution the argument holds the result.\nby_reference: the positions of the arguments whose arrays, of the target's array type, are the executable's storage, where bind! copies the others into storage it owns.\npoint: bind inputs as point! does, the caller's arrays the storage, as compile prepares.\nrecompute, ride: folds pinned to be recomputed where they are read, and values pinned to be computed as a recurrence's steps run, by their numbers as explain prints them: what the cover would otherwise decide by cost.\npartition: whether a minimum cut decides which of the forward's maps the backward recomputes rather than reads; false saves every map the backward reads.\nbuild: false plans and decides but builds and allocates nothing: the executable can be explained, not run.\nverify: check every plan the preparation forms and the storage of the one it runs (see Tempest.Compiler.verify); the default where the environment sets TEMPEST_VERIFY=true.\n\nA training loop, where the step returns the loss and the new parameters and optimizer state:\n\nex = prepare(trace(step, params, state, batch), params, state, batch; replaces = (2 => 1, 3 => 2))\ncapture!(ex)\nloss = first(outputs(ex))       # where each step leaves the loss\nfor batch in batches\n    bind!(ex, 3 => batch)       # only the batch is new\n    replay!(ex)                 # parameters and state are updated where they are\n    @show loss[]\nend\n\nA function that writes its arguments needs none of this: compile writes them where the caller has them, for any arrays a call brings.\n\nThe manual's page Preparing a program says how decisions are kept and replayed, what a claim that fails to build leaves, and each keyword in full.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.prepare"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.bind!","page":"Beneath compile","text":"bind!(ex, inputs...)\nbind!(ex, position => input, ...)\n\nCopy inputs into the executable's input buffers: all of them, in the structure prepare took them, or those at the positions given, as a new batch while parameters stay where the last execution left them. An array bound by reference and bound again is not copied.\n\njulia> ex = prepare(trace(x -> 2 .* x, [1.0, 2.0]), [1.0, 2.0]);\n\njulia> bind!(ex, [3.0, 4.0]);\n\njulia> outputs(execute!(ex))\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 6.0\n 8.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.bind!"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.point!","page":"Beneath compile","text":"point!(ex, inputs...) -> ex\n\nBind inputs as compile's call does: an argument's storage is the caller's array, read and written where it is, when every launch can be pointed elsewhere (repoints) and the target can read and write the array where it is (pointable); the array is copied otherwise, as bind! copies, into a buffer of the executable's own made the first time one is, and each execute! or replay! after copies what the program wrote there back into it. An argument bound by reference keeps its array. An array the storage is already costs nothing. The steps that use the storage of one elsewhere, the kernels that read or write it and the copies of saved inputs and staged results, are pointed at it, the results over it are over it, and a captured graph is changed where it records their addresses (retarget!), or recorded again where the target cannot change it; recording again costs more than copying an array that moves every call, as a training step's weights do, so an argument whose graph was recorded again is the caller's array only once the caller brings the same array twice in a row. The executable holds the arrays it reads and writes until it is bound again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.point!"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.execute!","page":"Beneath compile","text":"execute!(ex) -> ex\n\nRun the program on the inputs bound last: copy the inputs it writes into the results that are them as they were before, launch every kernel in order, then copy staged results into the arguments they replace, and what it wrote into an argument into the caller's array point! bound it to, where that is not its storage. The results are in outputs(ex). Whether or not the program is captured, this launches kernel by kernel; replay! runs the capture.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.execute!"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.capture!","page":"Beneath compile","text":"capture!(ex; repetitions = 1) -> ex\n\nRecord what execute! launches, for replay! to run, each replay repetitions executions of the program (for timing): as one graph on one device, and over several as a graph per run of the schedule of the executions, the runs of a device's steps and of a lane's crossings (see Captured). Captured runs nothing. What varies between replays lives in device buffers: inputs bound by copy or by reference, scalars and seeds among them, are read where they are, so they need no new capture. Capturing again replaces the graph. On a target without graphs, a replay launches the kernels.\n\nex = capture!(prepare(p, CuArray(x)))\nreplay!(ex)                     # runs the graph\nbind!(ex, CuArray(y))           # copied into the buffers the graph reads\nreplay!(ex)                     # the same graph, on the new values\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.capture!"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.replay!","page":"Beneath compile","text":"The nodes of program p recorded on inputs, handles of its arguments: a handle of each of its results.\n\n\n\n\n\nreplay!(ex) -> ex\n\nRun what capture! recorded, on the inputs bound last: what execute! does, repetitions times over, as one launch of a graph on a target with graphs, then copy what it wrote into an argument into the caller's array point! bound it to, where that is not its storage. The results are in outputs(ex).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.replay!"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.outputs","page":"Beneath compile","text":"outputs(ex) -> results\n\nWhere the executable leaves its results, in the program's output structure: arrays over its buffers, the same arrays every time, which each execute! or replay! fills again. Copy what you keep: a read or a copy of one, on any task, waits for the run that filled it, and the next run for the copy (see taken!). A number is an array of no dimensions here, since it lives in a buffer too; compile returns numbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.outputs"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.forget_session!","page":"Beneath compile","text":"forget_session!()\n\nEmpty every table of answers the session keeps (its compiled kernels by content, the functions loaded from them, which functions cuTile lowers, what identifies each package's code, among others), so that a preparation after finds each again, as one in a new process would. Julia's compiled code, the device's resources and the stores on disk stay: a new TEMPEST_CACHE and JULIA_OBJCACHE_PATH leave those behind too. The process's counts (cache_counts, reached_counts) stay, which are read by their differences. Each table's answer is keyed by everything it reads, so that emptying them costs time and never gives another answer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.forget_session!"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.precompile_calls","page":"Beneath compile","text":"precompile_calls(T)\n\nCompile the entry points a call of a program prepared for target type T takes: binding its arguments (point!), the call itself, capturing its launches (capture!), building a group's choice, and resolving a kernel from the cache. A target's extension calls it while it precompiles, since no workload calls a program where there is no device: compiled there, with the target's methods defined, rather than in every session that calls a program.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.precompile_calls"},{"category":"type","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.Plan","page":"Beneath compile","text":"A program's plan: which values are stored, and the groups its kernels compute, in order. A proposal is handed one, and reads its groups through group_views.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Plan"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.group_views","page":"Beneath compile","text":"group_views(plan) -> Vector{GroupView}\ngroup_views(prepared) -> Vector{GroupView}\n\nThe views of a plan's groups, or of the groups of the plan a prepared program runs, in the plan's order: what a proposal reads of the plan it is given, and what a package's precompile workload claims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.group_views"},{"category":"function","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.verify","page":"Beneath compile","text":"verify(plan) -> plan\nverify(plan, storage; in_place = k -> true) -> plan\n\nCheck the invariants the kernels of plan rely on, and throw an InvalidPlan naming the value and the kernel at the first that fails:\n\na stored value is a member of one kernel's group, and what a kernel or a result reads is an input, a constant, or stored earlier in the order;\nwith storage, no slot is given to a value while the value it holds is still to be used, each pair the storage records holds by the rule that allows it, and each lifetime covers its value's kernel and every reader;\na recurrence's group has the states of its cycle first and stores the cycle's values, and its other members ride its steps;\neach member's term loads values stored apart, each member has a place within the domain, a fold read as a Stat is complete in an earlier phase, and a fold a term recomputes is no member of the group;\na kernel's members and what it reads are on its device, but for a crossing, which reads one span of a value on another;\na kernel's domain has positions, no axis of it of extent zero.\n\nThe manual's page Plan invariants states each in full.\n\nin_place(k) says whether kernel k's realization reads each element a set of its outputs needs before it writes any of them, as storage was told. prepare checks every plan it forms, and its storage, with verify = true, the default when the environment sets TEMPEST_VERIFY=true, as the tests do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.verify"},{"category":"type","location":"compiler/#Tempest.Compiler.InvalidPlan","page":"Beneath compile","text":"InvalidPlan\n\nA plan, or its storage, that breaks one of the invariants verify checks: the invariant's number there, and what breaks it, naming the value and the kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.InvalidPlan"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Compiling-and-calling","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"compile(f, args...) traces f on args and prepares it, to be called like f: compile(f, x)(y) computes f(y) for any y alike x in structure, shapes and element types. It returns what f returns, in arrays of its own, with numbers as numbers, and what f writes into an argument's arrays it leaves there, as f would.\n\njulia> c = compile(x -> sum(abs2, x), [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]);\n\njulia> c([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n30.0\n\nA function that writes its arguments is compiled as it is written, and its state stays where the caller keeps it:\n\njulia> f!(s, x) = (s .= 0.5 .* s .+ x; sum(s));\n\njulia> s = [1.0, 2.0]; c = compile(f!, s, [0.0, 0.0]);\n\njulia> c(s, [1.0, 1.0]), s\n(3.5, [1.5, 2.0])","title":"Compiling and calling"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Inputs-and-constants","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"Which leaves of the arguments are inputs follows trace: arrays, also inside tuples, named tuples, structs, mutable ones too, and vectors of other things than numbers, whose lengths a call must match. An array the function reads inside any other leaf, a dictionary's say, would be a constant, and is refused, naming the argument.\n\nNumbers are constants; a number that varies between calls is an array, [x] say.\n\nf is an argument like the others, the call f(args...) traced: the arrays of a callable struct's fields and those a closure captures are inputs too, read at each call, so a closure over a model sees its weights as they are when called.","title":"Inputs and constants"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Globals","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"A global that f reads is no part of f, and tracing reads it once. One holding arrays is refused, since the program would keep them as they were when traced: pass it as an argument.\n\nIn f's own code a global is seen by its name; in a function f calls, by its arrays: an array tracing meets that lives in a global, of any module, is refused alike, as is a global a function assigned while traced.\n\nAny other global f's own code reads is a constant, and one not declared const is checked at each call. A number a function f calls reads, or a copy it makes of a global's array before using it with an input, is kept as it was when traced.","title":"Globals"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Where-the-arrays-live","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"An argument's array is the program's storage for it, read and written where the caller has it, not copied: a call on the arrays of the call before costs nothing for them, and one on others changes the addresses the captured graph's kernels read and write, in place (see point!), so a training step whose weights are new arrays at every call copies none of them.\n\nThe program is built on the arrays compile is given and holds no storage of its own for them, so a model's weights are in memory once; it holds the arrays of its last call, or until the first those it was compiled on, until a call brings others.\n\nWhat f writes into an argument the kernels write into the caller's array: where the kernel that writes it reads the array only at the addresses it writes, and the kernels that read it otherwise can run first, directly, as an append to a cache writes only its new positions; else into storage of the program's own and then copied into the array once, after the last kernel (explain says which).\n\nEverything else the program stores lives in buffers it owns and reuses at every call. An array not of the target's array type, an Array for a function compiled for the GPU say, is copied into a buffer the program makes the first time it needs one and keeps, and, if written, back after the call.","title":"Where the arrays live"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#What-a-call-must-match","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"A call whose arguments, f's own leaves or the globals f reads differ in anything but the values of inputs throws, naming the difference. So does a call whose arrays share memory with one f writes: the program reads each array apart, where f run on them would read what it wrote.","title":"What a call must match"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Measuring","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"Unless asked, nothing is timed: the first call runs the plan the estimates pick, with the times the tuning record holds, and the plan does not change after. Two keywords ask for more.\n\nmeasure says which claims are timed, as for prepare:\n\nmeasure what is timed\n:none, the default nothing\n:close the options whose estimates are close\n:all every option\n\ntrue and false mean :close and :none.\n\nsearch says when, where something is timed:\n\nsearch when\n:before, the default while preparing, so that the first call runs the plan measurement picks\n:after behind the first result\n\nWhere nothing is timed, with measure = :none or on a target that times nothing, there is nothing to search and search means nothing, as measure means nothing on such a target. The environment overrides the defaults, TEMPEST_MEASURE (none, close or all) and TEMPEST_SEARCH (before or after), and a keyword given overrides the environment.","title":"Measuring"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Decisions-kept","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"Measuring once serves the preparations after it. A preparation that measures close calls keeps the decisions its cover settles, and one that measures nothing, in this process or another, replays them: its plan is the measured one, with nothing timed. That holds for\n\nthe same program, the same arguments' shapes and types and the same f with what it captures,\non the same target and device,\nwith the same toolchain's record and rates file (a file per device and toolchain) still holding the times those decisions rest on,\nwhere the measuring preparation settled (its covers, up to three, ended on one that measured nothing new; TEMPEST_SETTLE=false keeps unsettled decisions, which are not replayed so),\nand no claim failed to build in the preparation that replays.\n\nDecisions reached measuring every option are not replayed by one that measures nothing, and none are kept or replayed with TEMPEST_REPLAY=false (see prepare).","title":"Decisions kept"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Searching-behind-the-first-result","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"With search = :after, compile returns a Searching, called as the Compiled is, with the plan the estimates pick, prepared timing nothing, while a task (a Tempest.Compiler.Search) prepares the program again, measuring as measure says, its decisions settled as a preparation's are.\n\nIt compiles and builds on a thread of its own where Julia has more than one; on one, it runs only where a call waits, and holds the calls while it compiles, and wait(c) after the first result takes the whole search at once.\n\nThe calls take its timings, one each after its own work, on the calling task: so a claim is timed with none of the program's own work beside it, as when preparing, and the search never captures a graph beside the caller's work, which CUDA forbids the device's synchronization during. Other programs' work in the process is not held off while a claim is timed, as it is not while preparing.\n\nThe first call after the search ends runs its plan, captured then, as compile captures the first, where it differs. A program not called searches no further than its next timing; wait(c) takes the search's timings until it ends, and changes the plan.\n\nThe record keeps every time taken, so a search cut short loses none, and the decisions it settles are kept as it ends: a preparation of the same program after it replays them, its first result is the measured plan, and there is nothing to search behind it, so compile returns a Compiled, as it does where nothing is timed. A search that fails leaves the program its plan, with a warning.\n\nTwo plans of a program round alike to the program's precision but not to the bit: a step's results may change in their last bits from the call that runs the new plan, as between two preparations.","title":"Searching behind the first result"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#Capture","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"With capture, the program's launches are recorded once as one graph that each call replays, as capture! and replay! do. Results are the same either way.","title":"Capture"},{"category":"section","location":"compiling/#The-level-beneath","page":"Compiling and calling","text":"The level beneath is in Tempest.Compiler: prepare, bind!, and execute! or replay!, with point! binding a call's arrays as compile does. c.executable is the prepared program, and explain(c) shows it.","title":"The level beneath"},{"category":"section","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement","page":"Implement","text":"","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement"},{"category":"section","location":"implement/#Libraries","page":"Implement","text":"A library realizes the groups that compute a function Tempest can already compute. It names that function as a template, and Tempest finds the groups that compute it and hands the library each array as a strided view.","title":"Libraries"},{"category":"module","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement","page":"Implement","text":"The interface kernel packages implement. An implementation realizes groups of a plan as kernels: it claims the groups it can realize, estimates the launches of each claim, and builds them. It may also propose groups of its own. An implementation is a value, and a target holds those it uses (see CUDATarget). Claims read a GroupView of the group, in the vocabulary of Tempest.Compiler.IR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Implementation","page":"Implement","text":"An implementation realizes groups of a plan as kernels. claims returns the ways it can realize a group, estimate the launches of each, and build_claim builds them. A target's floor realizes any group, so a plan never depends on an implementation. claims, variants and estimate answer of their arguments alone, and the cover asks them for many groups side by side, on the threads there are: what they keep between calls, as a trial compilation or a library's graph, they keep in a table a lock guards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Implementation"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.package_of","page":"Implement","text":"package_of(implementation) -> Symbol\n\nThe package whose kernels implementation launches, by name: the rates file keeps the line and the memory it is priced on under it, shared by the package's implementations. By default the module that defines its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.package_of"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.loaded","page":"Implement","text":"loaded(T) -> Bool\n\nWhether implementations of type T can be made: true but for Tempest's own implementations of a package, whose extension says so when the package loads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.loaded"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.GroupView","page":"Implement","text":"GroupView\n\nA group of the plan as implementations see it. A group computes its members over one domain: reductions, its folds, and outputs, its other values, each defined by its term in terms; a fold may cover fewer of the domain's positions (see own_domain). A :recurrence group's outputs are a recurrence's state, its term where the state starts; the values of its cycle it stores, in order; and last the value the state is carried from, the state one step on, stored only where something else reads it. Each step computes them in order, reading what it stored, over the axes their placements mention (see recurrence_axes). A :scan group's reductions are scans along its primary_axes in one direction, which its members read at the position its sweep has reached. Folds over primary_axes, the axes its first fold not by key reduces, can be read within the group, and phase counts the folds each member waits for. values describes every value the group computes or reads, placement where it keeps each member (its index over the domain, or nothing), and bytes what it reads from storage and writes. reference(target, buffers) builds the group's reference kernels, the floor's, which every claim agrees with. Its structure is the rest in its own numbering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.GroupView"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.ValueView","page":"Implement","text":"What a view holds of a value: its element type and dims, whether it is a number rather than an array (stored as one element), and whether it is stored; for a fold, the fold and the axes of its domain it reduces, and whether it is a fold by key (keyed), which reduces every axis and combines each element at its placement, after into where it is given: the contents it starts from, a term over its own domain; for a scan its fold, no axes, and scan: whether it runs from the last element down (reverse) and leaves each element out of its own prefix (exclusive), nothing for any other value; and for a value masked by formulas over its positions, masked: the formulas, each at most zero where the mask holds, and the number the value is where it fails (see Tempest.Compiler.IR.comparison_mask and Tempest.Compiler.IR.masked_result); observed: formulas over its positions, each at most zero where the program's results may depend on what it holds, nothing when they may depend on all of it (see Tempest.Compiler.observed); and for a recurrence's state, carried: the axis it steps along, the value it is carried from, a load over its domain read one step back, and whether it steps from the axis's last position to its first, reading one step on (reverse; see Tempest.Compiler.Carried).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.ValueView"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.members","page":"Implement","text":"The values a group computes, its folds first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.members"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.own_domain","page":"Implement","text":"own_domain(group, m) -> extents\n\nThe positions member m of a group covers: the group's domain, or, for a fold over fewer positions along axes it keeps, its own within the domain, as MLA's dV, over the values' 64 features, in the group of dK and dQ, over the 96 of the queries and keys. Such a fold's term is a term over the domain, but only at its own positions does it read within what it reads, and only there is it kept; nothing in the group reads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.own_domain"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.product_axes","page":"Implement","text":"product_axes(group) -> (M, N, K, B) or nothing\nproduct_axes(group, r) -> (M, N, K, B) or nothing\n\nThe axes of a group whose folds each sum a product of two factors over the group's primary axes K: those only the first factor reads (M), those only the second reads (N), and those both read and keep (B). Nothing when a fold is not such a product, or the folds split the axes differently; a fold of weights alone, a log-sum-exp beside attention's weighted sum, has no factors and rides the products' sweep. Given a fold r, the axes of that fold's product alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.product_axes"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.structure","page":"Implement","text":"structure(group) -> Structure\n\nAll the view holds but reference, each value numbered by where it first appears: its members first, in order, then the values their placements and terms read, then those the values' own views name. Groups of one structure are one group but for their values' numbers, as a model's layers are: what an implementation answers of one (claims, variants, estimate) it answers of the others, where its claims name no value, and it may keep its answers by it (see Tempest.Compiler.SessionTable). Found once a view.\n\n\n\n\n\nTempest.Compiler.structure(plan) -> (; kernels, stored, bytes)\n\nWhat tests compare between plans: the number of kernels, the values written to storage (inputs and constants apart), and their bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.structure"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Structure","page":"Implement","text":"Structure\n\nA group in its own numbering (see structure): equal to another only where the two are one group but for the numbers of their values. Its functions, folds and element types are objects, which a function redefined stays: what they are as code is code(structure), which a table kept longer than one preparation names too (see Tempest.Compiler.definitions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Structure"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.numbering","page":"Implement","text":"numbering(group) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe values of the group by their number in its structure: what an implementation that keeps what it made for one group by its structure, its kernels say, reads to give another group of the structure its own values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.numbering"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.code","page":"Implement","text":"code(s::Structure) -> Vector{Any}\n\nThe code the parts of s name, each once: the functions its terms apply, its folds and its values' element types. Structures equal name the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.code"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.recurrence_axes","page":"Implement","text":"recurrence_axes(g::GroupView) -> (; axis, parallel, inner)\n\nA :recurrence group's axes: the state's axis it steps along; those of the state's along which no term reads what the group computes at another position, its items' (parallel); and the rest, which each step computes whole (inner).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.recurrence_axes"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.claims","page":"Implement","text":"claims(implementation, target, group) -> claims\n\nThe ways implementation can realize group, a GroupView, on target, each a value of its own type that estimate and build_claim take; empty when it cannot, as by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.claims"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.variants","page":"Implement","text":"variants(implementation, target, group, claim) -> claims\n\nThe claims that vary claim, offered once it has measured best among the claims tried on group so far: the next stage of a search the implementation stages, as the generator stages its product tiles (every output tile at one depth, then the depths of the best and its split). The cover tries them in turn until the best has none new. None by default: then claims is the whole search.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.variants"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.estimate","page":"Implement","text":"estimate(implementation, claim, target, group) -> Vector{Work}\n\nThe launches of a claim. By default one launch moving the group's bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.estimate"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Work","page":"Implement","text":"Work(bytes; reread = 0, passed = 0, flops = 0, recomputed = 0, factor = Float32, blocks = Inf, split = false, applied = [], swept = [], inner = [], steps = 0, step_runs = [], stepped = [], refold_steps = 0)\n\nOne launch's work, which Tempest prices (see the manual's page A launch's work):\n\nbytes: bytes moved;\nreread: bytes it reads again, what its blocks read of an argument beyond the first time;\npassed: bytes it passes to or takes from another of its claim's launches, partial results in a workspace, as a split sum's parts;\nflops, factor: flops of products whose factors are of type factor;\nrecomputed: flops of folds it recomputes inside them;\nblocks, split: its blocks, and whether they split the sums among them;\napplied: the scalar functions it applies elementwise, each with the number of positions it is applied at;\nswept: those applied inside a product's sweep, to its factors;\ninner: those applied inside a fold the sweep recomputes, to that fold's tiles;\nsteps: the steps a block takes, where its blocks sweep tiles one after another, waiting for what each step loads before the next;\nstep_runs: what a block loads at a step: of each load, the bytes it reads in one run, contiguous, paired with the bytes it reads in the step;\nstepped: the functions the steps compute on what they loaded, each with the number of steps it is applied in along their chain;\nrefold_steps: the steps apart in which a block recomputes a fold whose body is not bilinear along its axis.\n\nA launch that states no steps, or one, is priced by its bytes, flops and functions alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Work"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.resources","page":"Implement","text":"resources(implementation, target) -> resources\n\nWhat the launches implementation builds for one executable share, and nothing else uses: memory a launch writes only while it runs, as a library's workspace, made on target (allocate, upload) as the executable's buffers are. An executable runs one call at a time, its launches in order, so its launches may share it; another executable's, or a decider's timing claims beside it, have their own. Made once per executable, and once per decider for the claims it builds to time, when the first launch is built; nothing by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.resources"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.build_claim","page":"Implement","text":"build_claim(implementation, claim, target, group, buffers, resources) -> (launch, description)\n\nBuild a claim: launch() runs its kernels, and description says what they are. buffers[v] is the storage of value v, for each value the group reads or stores, and resources what resources(implementation, target) made for the executable the launch is built for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.build_claim"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.repoints","page":"Implement","text":"repoints(launch) -> Bool\n\nWhether launch, as build_claim returned it, can read a value from other storage than it was built on (see repoint!): false by default. compile points the launches of an argument the program only reads at the caller's array, rather than copying it into the program's storage, when all of the program's launches can.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.repoints"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.repoint!","page":"Implement","text":"repoint!(launch, value, buffer)\n\nMake launch read value value, a number among the plan's values as in build_claim's buffers, from buffer from now on, where it read the storage it was given before; a launch that does not read it is unchanged. buffer is the target's storage of that value's element type and length, aligned as the target's own storage is to the bytes its kernels assume of an address. A graph captured from the launch is captured again before it runs on buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.repoint!"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.candidates","page":"Implement","text":"candidates(implementation, target, plan) -> Vector\n\nWhat the implementation proposes in place of the generator's groups: sets of values to realize as one group each, grown from anchors in plan (a product) and accepted by the implementation, or several such sets, each a group of its own, proposed as one. None by default. Proposals read the compiler's plan, its groups through Tempest.Compiler.group_views.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.candidates"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.record_name","page":"Implement","text":"A claim's name in the tuning record, without value numbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.record_name"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.recomputes_folds","page":"Implement","text":"Whether implementation realizes groups that recompute folds where they read them, on target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.recomputes_folds"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.in_place","page":"Implement","text":"in_place(implementation, claim) -> Bool\n\nWhether the kernels of claim read every element that the outputs of one shape need before they write any of them, so that an output may be written into the storage of a value they read only at the address they write it (see storage). The floor's kernels do, and so do the generator's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.in_place"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.lowers","page":"Implement","text":"lowers(implementation, target, term, group) -> Bool\n\nWhether implementation can realize a group that applies term, an Apply in group, a GroupView: false for a generator without a lowering of the scalar function. What an implementation cannot do is declared here, before it claims, rather than learned from a build that fails; the cover stores a map the target's implementations cannot all lower apart, in a kernel of its own (see Tempest.Compiler.Store). True by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.lowers"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.compiling","page":"Implement","text":"compiling() -> Bool\n\nWhether an implementation may compile on the device while it claims, as a generator does to learn what it lowers (see lowers): not while a preparation that builds nothing plans, which touches no device, nor while a package precompiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.compiling"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Library","page":"Implement","text":"A library realizes the groups that compute one of its templates on a target: Tempest matches them and hands supports and build a Binding of the template's arrays. claims, estimate, build_claim, record_name and candidates are Tempest's for a library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Library"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Template","page":"Implement","text":"Template(f; inputs, outputs)\n\nA function a library computes, f, of arrays named and with named axes in inputs, as (A = (:m, :k), B = (:k, :n)), returning the arrays named in outputs, one name for each, in order. Tempest traces f with arrays of the axes' extents; an axis no axis of a group matches has extent one. An axis stands for the group's axes that the same arrays read, the same folds sum and the same index formulas (a causal mask's) move with. Axes alike in all three are interchangeable, as attention's query positions and the query heads that share a key head are: the library is offered a binding for each way, those whose arrays are laid out as inputs names their axes first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Template"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.templates","page":"Implement","text":"templates(library, target) -> templates\n\nThe functions library computes on target, a tuple of Templates; none by default, so that a library computes nothing on a target it does not name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.templates"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.supports","page":"Implement","text":"supports(library, target, binding) -> Bool\nsupports(library, target, bindings::Vector{Binding}) -> Bool\n\nWhether library computes binding on target: the element types, extents and strides it takes; true by default. Given bindings, instances of one template that a group computes side by side, alike but for where their arrays start, as products of chunks of one value are: whether it computes them all in one launch; false by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.supports"},{"category":"function","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.build","page":"Implement","text":"build(library, target, binding, resources) -> (launch, description)\nbuild(library, target, bindings::Vector{Binding}, resources) -> (launch, description)\n\nWhat build_claim is for a library: launch() computes the template into the binding's outputs from its inputs, each Strided with its buffer, on the task's stream, or every instance of bindings it supports, using the executable's resources (see resources), and description says what it runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.build"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Binding","page":"Implement","text":"A group matched to the templateth of a library's templates: the extent of each of the template's axes in extents, and each of its inputs and outputs as a Strided view in arrays, by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Binding"},{"category":"type","location":"implement/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Strided","page":"Implement","text":"An array of a binding: elements of eltype at offset and the given strides, in elements, along axes of extents size. buffer is the storage it is a view of when the library builds, and nothing when it is asked whether it supports the binding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.Strided"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Index-formulas","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Index formulas"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Layout","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Folds","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Folds"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Terms","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Terms"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Masks","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Masks"},{"category":"section","location":"ir/#Tuples-a-program-makes","page":"IR","text":"","title":"Tuples a program makes"},{"category":"module","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR","page":"IR","text":"The vocabulary the compiler's levels share and implementations read: index expressions over a domain, how values are laid out in storage, the algebra of folds, the terms of a kernel's body, and the masks index formulas state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Domain","page":"IR","text":"Domain(extents)\n\nThe variables an expression ranges over: variable n takes the values 0:extents[n]-1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Domain"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Var","page":"IR","text":"The nth variable of the enclosing domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Var"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Affine","page":"IR","text":"constant + Σ coefficient·atom, atoms sorted, coefficients nonzero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Affine"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Div","page":"IR","text":"Floor division of an expression by a positive constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Div"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Mod","page":"IR","text":"The remainder, in 0:d-1, of an expression divided by a positive constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Mod"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Indirect","page":"IR","text":"An integer read from value at index, known to lie in lo:hi: a gather.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Indirect"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.is_affine","page":"IR","text":"True when e is constant + Σ cₙ·xₙ with no division, modulo or gather.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.is_affine"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.indirect_values","page":"IR","text":"The values an expression gathers from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.indirect_values"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.substitute","page":"IR","text":"substitute(e, arguments, domain)\n\nReplace variable n of e by arguments[n], an expression over domain, and simplify there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.substitute"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.substituted","page":"IR","text":"substituted(index, arguments, domain)\n\nEach expression of index, a tuple of them, with variable n replaced by arguments[n], as substitute replaces it: by code compiled once whatever the ranks of the two, which are a program's (the index of a value of any rank, read by a domain of any).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.substituted"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.revalued","page":"IR","text":"revalued(e, number)\n\ne with the value each gather in it reads, value, numbered number(value) instead, its atoms sorted again.\n\n\n\n\n\nrevalued(t, number, renamed = Dict{Term, Term}()) -> Term\n\nt with each value it names, as a load, a fold's statistic, a fold it recomputes or a gather in an index does, numbered number(value) instead. Each subterm is renamed once (renamed), so that a term sharing its parts is walked as the graph it is, in the order a walk of t first reaches them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.revalued"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.fdiv","page":"IR","text":"fdiv(e, k, domain)\n\nfld(e, k) for a positive constant k, simplified: terms divisible by k come out of the division, a remainder known to lie in 0:k-1 vanishes, and a factor common to the other terms and k divides out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.fdiv"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.fmod","page":"IR","text":"fmod(e, k, domain)\n\nmod(e, k) for a positive constant k, simplified: terms divisible by k vanish, a remainder known to lie in 0:k-1 is returned as it is, and a factor common to the other terms and k comes out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.fmod"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.storage_length","page":"IR","text":"The elements value v stores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.storage_length"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.address","page":"IR","text":"address(v, index, domain) -> Affine\n\nThe position of index, a formula over domain per axis of value v, in the dense storage of v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.address"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.group_stride","page":"IR","text":"group_stride(e, axes, domain) -> stride or nothing\n\nThe stride of address e along axes collapsed into one axis (the first fastest), or nothing when its coefficients do not chain over them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.group_stride"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Relative","page":"IR","text":"Relative(law, observe)\n\nA fold relative to a statistic of its own elements, in one pass. An element is (y, g...), or a number y, which is (y, 1). The state is the statistic's state over the elements so far and one sum of g · φ(y - m) for each g, where m is the statistic and φ the law's function: for the Exponential law, exp(y - m) against the maximum; for the Square law, (y - m)^2 against the mean. Each sum is relative to the statistic of the elements it covers, and combining two states moves both to the statistic of their union by the law's rebase: exp turns a difference of maxima into a factor, and a sum of squares about a part's mean moves to the whole's by n (μ_part - μ)^2. So softmax and the variance are each one pass, with no statistic computed first. The result is observe(law, state).\n\nlogsumexp is the exponential fold of a number observed as m + log(sum) (LOGSUMEXP); the sums a softmax weighs, and the sums of squares about a mean, become relative folds by rewrite (see rewrite).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Relative"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Exponential","page":"IR","text":"exp(y - m) against the maximum m. A sum relative to one maximum moves to a larger one by the factor exp(m_old - m_new). Exponentials are shifted by zero when the maximum is infinite, so an empty or all -Inf slice sums to zero, and a +Inf element to +Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Exponential"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Square","page":"IR","text":"(y - m)^2 against the mean m, whose state is the count and the sum. A sum of squares about a part's mean moves to the mean of the whole by n_part (μ_part - μ)^2 (Welford's algorithm, as Chan combines it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Square"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Statistic","page":"IR","text":"The statistic itself: the maximum, or the mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Statistic"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Sum","page":"IR","text":"The ith sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Sum"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Composed","page":"IR","text":"f of other observes and numbers: Composed(/, (Sum(1), Sum(2))) is the quotient of two sums. Called with a state alone, it composes the observes of a GenericFold, functions of the state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Composed"},{"category":"constant","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.LOGSUMEXP","page":"IR","text":"The log-sum-exp: the maximum plus the log of the sum of exponentials relative to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.LOGSUMEXP"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.InSequence","page":"IR","text":"InSequence(fold)\n\nfold combined in sequence, as Base's accumulate combines a function it is given: from init, the state the sweep starts from, which need be no identity, one element after another, by a combine that claims no law.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.InSequence"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Overwrite","page":"IR","text":"Overwrite()\n\nThe fold of a write: its state is the element it took last. A write by key starts each position from the array's contents there, so its initial state, zero, is never observed where a write has contents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Overwrite"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.fold_reduce","page":"IR","text":"fold_reduce(f) -> function or nothing\n\nHow to fold arrays of states along some of their axes at once, when f has a faster way than combining them in pairs: a function of the state's components, one array each, and the axes, that returns the folded components. Implementations call it on their own arrays, such as tiles. A relative fold takes the statistic first, so that each sum is moved once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.fold_reduce"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.product_factors","page":"IR","text":"product_factors(fold, term) -> (x, y) or nothing\n\nThe two factors whose products a fold of term sums: those of a sum of a product; for an exponential fold of (y, g...), the first g that is not a constant, and y, whose exp(y - m) weighs it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.product_factors"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Load","page":"IR","text":"A stored value read at an index of the kernel's domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Load"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Ix","page":"IR","text":"An integer computed from the kernel's indices, converted to T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Ix"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Stat","page":"IR","text":"A reduction of the kernel, at the kernel's current position along its kept axes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Stat"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Recomputed","page":"IR","text":"A fold the kernel recomputes where it reads it, at index: the fold of value value, whose operand is body over the reader's domain extended by the fold's reduced axes, of extents, numbered after the domain's own variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Recomputed"},{"category":"type","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.Cast","page":"IR","text":"A conversion to T, as a primitive with its own derivative rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.Cast"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.mentions","page":"IR","text":"Whether term t depends on variable n of its domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.mentions"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.mentions_within","page":"IR","text":"Whether term t mentions no axis past the first n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.mentions_within"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.reached_elements","page":"IR","text":"reached_elements(ts, dims, domain; run = 1)\n\nThe elements of a value of dims that loads ts of it reach over domain: along each axis, the span of the positions their indices take there, one where they are held, as a slice's index is, and no more than the positions each index visits, one for each point of the domain's axes it mentions, those an index it gathers through mentions among them: a table's rows gathered at 64 tokens are 64, wherever in the table they lie. Along the first axis, whose elements lie side by side, a position reached brings in run of them, the elements memory moves at once. Every element where an index mentions a variable past the domain's, as the body of a fold recomputed inside does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.reached_elements"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.sector_elements","page":"IR","text":"The elements of type T memory moves at once: a sector's (see SECTOR_BYTES).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.sector_elements"},{"category":"constant","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.SECTOR_BYTES","page":"IR","text":"The bytes memory moves at once: a sector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.SECTOR_BYTES"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.comparison_mask","page":"IR","text":"comparison_mask(f, a, b, domain) -> Vector{Affine}\n\nThe mask that the comparison f(a, b) of index formulas states: formulas, each at most zero where it holds. a <= b is a - b <= 0, a < b is a - b + 1 <= 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.comparison_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.held_formula","page":"IR","text":"held_formula(value, index, T, domain) -> Affine or nothing\n\nThe integer of type T that value holds at index, as a formula: a gather known to lie in the type's range, so that a position compared with it is a mask. Nothing for a type wider than 32 bits, whose range the arithmetic of formulas, in Int, could overflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.held_formula"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.masked_result","page":"IR","text":"masked_result(f, T, args)\n\nWhat f gives, as a T, where a mask fails, from its arguments there, each a number, :finite or nothing (unknown): a number when the numbers decide it, as a zero factor or an infinite summand does, in exact arithmetic; for a choice by a known condition, the branch it takes, whatever the other is; :finite for sums, products, quotients by a nonzero number and the functions here of finite arguments, which an overflow could make infinite, so that a program whose unmasked positions overflow computes a different nothing here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.masked_result"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.map_elements","page":"IR","text":"map_elements(f, t::Tuple) -> Tuple\nmap_elements(f, a::Tuple, b::Tuple) -> Tuple\n\nmap(f, t), by code compiled once for every type of t: the walkers of a program's nested arguments meet tuples as long as the user makes them, and the planner indices, shapes and operands of a program's every rank and mix of kinds. Of two tuples, element by element, as many as the shorter has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.map_elements"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.all_elements","page":"IR","text":"all_elements(p, t::Tuple) -> Bool\n\nall(p, t), by code compiled once for every type of t, as map_elements maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.all_elements"},{"category":"function","location":"ir/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.any_element","page":"IR","text":"any_element(p, t::Tuple) -> Bool\n\nany(p, t), by code compiled once for every type of t, as map_elements maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.any_element"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Tempest","page":"Tempest","text":"","title":"Tempest"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Compiling-a-function","page":"Tempest","text":"","title":"Compiling a function"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Writing-programs","page":"Tempest","text":"Programs are written with Base: broadcasts, folds (mapreduce with a tuple state among them), scans (accumulate, cumsum), products, indexing and ranges (a range broadcast against a traced array is the index itself, a formula rather than an array). Beyond Base, programs can call logsumexp, and claim with a GenericFold the laws of a combine, which Base cannot spell:\n\nAny other function a program calls, a batched product say, is written in these and lives in its own package. Where its body does not trace, an extension gives it a method on traced arrays, as NNlibExt and EinopsExt do, with these:","title":"Writing programs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Derivative-rules","page":"Tempest","text":"","title":"Derivative rules"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Tempest","page":"Tempest","text":"Tempest compiles traced Julia array programs into planned, fused kernels.\n\nA program is Julia array code, and what it computes reaches the compiler as three things: maps, folds (a scan is a fold that keeps the axis it runs along, a scatter one that combines each element at a position it computes), and reads at index formulas. Products, reshapes, permutations, gathers and scatters are spellings of them, and so is every function a program calls. Tempest names only what Base cannot spell:\n\nthe way in: compile, explain, gradient and withgradient, the targets, and the implementations of kernel packages Tempest has extensions for;\nthe derivative of a scalar function, which its code does not carry: scalar_partials, and a value no gradient flows through: stopgrad;\nthe laws of a fold's combine: GenericFold;\nthe log-sum-exp as one fold, where a target that keeps the rounding as written would take its spelling in two passes: logsumexp.\n\nTempest.Compiler is how a program becomes kernels; its public names are what packages beside a program need: the step beneath compile, what an extension writes traced methods with, and what kernel packages and targets read and define. Compiler.IR is the vocabulary its levels share and implementations read, and Compiler.Implement the interface kernel packages implement.\n\nThe manual's page Programs says how folds and scans are read, and where any other function lives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.compile","page":"Tempest","text":"compile(f, args...; target, measure = :none, search = :before, capture = true) -> Compiled or Searching\n\nf traced on args and prepared, to be called like f: compile(f, x)(y) computes f(y) for any y alike x in structure, shapes and element types. It returns what f returns, in arrays of its own, with numbers as numbers, and what f writes into an argument's arrays it leaves there, as f would.\n\nThe arrays among the arguments are inputs, those f holds or captures among them, read and written where the caller has them; numbers are constants. A call that differs from the one compiled in anything but its inputs' values throws, naming the difference.\n\ntarget: where the program runs; that of the first array among f and the arguments that has one, else the CPU's.\nmeasure: which claims are timed: :none, the default, none, so that the plan is the one the estimates pick; :close, the options whose estimates are close; :all, every option. true and false mean :close and :none.\nsearch: when they are timed: :before, the default, while preparing, so that the first call runs the plan measurement picks; :after, behind the first result, where compile returns a Searching.\ncapture: whether the program's launches are recorded once as one graph that each call replays. Results are the same either way.\n\nThe environment overrides the defaults of measure and search, TEMPEST_MEASURE (none, close or all) and TEMPEST_SEARCH (before or after), and a keyword given overrides the environment.\n\njulia> c = compile(x -> sum(abs2, x), [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]);\n\njulia> c([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n30.0\n\nThe manual's page Compiling and calling says which leaves are inputs, how globals are read, where the program keeps its arrays, what a call must match, and how measuring and searching go. The level beneath is in Tempest.Compiler: prepare, bind!, and execute! or replay!. c.executable is the prepared program, and explain(c) shows it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.compile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.Compiled","page":"Tempest","text":"A function traced and prepared by compile. Calls run one at a time, from any task. c.executable is the prepared program the calls run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Compiled"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.wait-Tuple{Tempest.Compiler.Compiled}","page":"Tempest","text":"wait(c::Compiled) -> c\n\nAt once: a compiled function searches nothing behind its calls (see compile's search, which returns a Searching that does).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.wait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.Searching","page":"Tempest","text":"A function compiled with its search behind the first result (see compile's search), called as a Compiled is: s.compiled is the one the calls run, its plan the estimates' until the search ends and the first call after runs the search's, where it differs. Each call takes one of the search's timings after its own work; wait(s) takes the rest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Searching"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.wait-Tuple{Tempest.Compiler.Searching}","page":"Tempest","text":"wait(s::Searching) -> s\n\nTake the timings of the search behind s's first result until it ends, and give the calls its plan where it differs (see compile's search); at once where the calls have taken it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.wait"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.gradient","page":"Tempest","text":"Tempest.gradient(f, args...) -> gradients\n\nThe gradient of f(args...), a number, with respect to each argument, a tuple with one per argument, in that argument's structure: an array for each floating-point array, and nothing for anything else. Numbers are constants, among a gradient's arguments as among a program's, so a number to differentiate by is an array, [x] say. A struct's gradient is a named tuple of its fields', and a vector's a vector of its elements'. It is Zygote's gradient, and withgradient returns the value too.\n\nIn traced code it differentiates f where it is called, so a training step is a function like any other, traced and prepared as one program (see withgradient). What f closes over is a constant to the gradient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.withgradient","page":"Tempest","text":"Tempest.withgradient(f, args...) -> (val = value, grad = gradients)\n\nThe value of f(args...), a number, and its gradient with respect to each argument, as gradient returns it, as Zygote's withgradient does. On Julia arrays this is interpret(differentiate(trace(f, args...)), args...).\n\nIn traced code it differentiates f where it is called, so a training step is a function like any other, traced and prepared as one program:\n\nfunction step(params, state, batch)\n    loss, (grads,) = Tempest.withgradient(p -> loss_of(p, batch), params)\n    new_params, new_state = adam(params, state, grads)\n    (loss, new_params, new_state)\nend\n\nWhat f closes over is a constant to the gradient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.withgradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.explain","page":"Tempest","text":"explain([io], c::Compiled)\nexplain([io], ex::Executable)\nexplain([io], plan)\n\nPrint what Tempest decided for a program. For each kernel: what it computes, what it reads, and which values it stores or leaves to be recomputed where they are read. For a prepared program, also the precision of its products, the close calls measured between implementations, how values share storage, which implementation runs each kernel, at what estimated or measured time, and the claims on it that failed to build.\n\nIt describes what Tempest decided today; what it prints may change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.explain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.CPUTarget","page":"Tempest","text":"CPUTarget(implementations...)\n\nRun on the CPU, through the same generated floor code CUDA runs, and the implementations given, none by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.CPUTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.CUDATarget","page":"Tempest","text":"CUDATarget(implementations...; device = nothing, precision = :full, online = true, rounded_backward = true, deterministic = false)\n\nRun on NVIDIA GPUs; needs CUDACore loaded. A program runs where its arrays are, whatever device the calling task is on. The target uses the floor and the implementations given, as in CUDATarget(cuTileGenerator()), ties between their estimates going to the first; by default Tempest's own whose packages are loaded when it is made, cuBLAS's, cuDNN's and cuTile's.\n\ndevice: the target's home, as CUDA numbers devices from 0: where arrays on the host go, and where claims are timed. By default the first device an argument is on, or, with every array on the host, the calling task's.\nprecision: the numerics policy for products: :full keeps Float32 products in Float32, :tf32 allows TF32 tensor cores for them, and :tf32x3 splits each Float32 factor into two TF32 parts and sums three tensor-core products (close to Float32 accuracy).\nonline: permits the online softmax: a sum weighted by a softmax accumulates in one pass with the softmax's maximum, rescaled as it grows, which changes rounding.\nrounded_backward: lets a backward read a forward's sum as the program rounds it to half precision, where the sums of the derivative are exchanged, rather than in Float32.\ndeterministic: keeps a sum's rounding from depending on the order its blocks run in.\n\nThe manual's page The CUDA target says how half-precision products round, and what rounded_backward and deterministic cost and gain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.CUDATarget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.cuBLASProducts","page":"Tempest","text":"cuBLASProducts()\n\nProducts as cuBLAS's batched ones, of Float32 factors or half ones widened to Float32, and products alike but for where their operands start as one call. Needs cuBLAS loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.cuBLASProducts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.cuDNNAttention","page":"Tempest","text":"cuDNNAttention()\n\nAttention of half-precision queries, keys and values as cuDNN's SDPA engines compute it, with and without a causal mask. Needs cuDNN loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.cuDNNAttention"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.Implement.cuTileGenerator","page":"Tempest","text":"cuTileGenerator()\n\nTempest's groups as cuTile kernels: the generator, which claims every group it can tile and lower, and searches its tiles. Needs cuTile loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Implement.cuTileGenerator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.logsumexp","page":"Tempest","text":"Tempest.logsumexp(x; dims = :)\n\nlog.(sum(exp.(x); dims)), computed stably in one pass: of integers, of the floats exp takes them to, as the fold relative to the maximum holds over floats (see relative_over).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.logsumexp"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.IR.GenericFold","page":"Tempest","text":"GenericFold(; init, combine, lift = tuple, observe = only, pullback = nothing)\n\nA fold from functions, given to reduce or accumulate where the operator goes. lift maps an element to a state tuple, combine merges two, the earlier first, and observe maps the last state to the result; init is a state tuple or a function of the element type returning one. It claims what Base cannot spell: that combine associates and commutes, up to rounding, and init is its identity, so a reduction or a scan by it may group its elements in any order. A function given to accumulate is combined in sequence instead. pullback(x, y, g, axes), when given, is the derivative rule of a reduction by it.\n\njulia> recurrence = Tempest.GenericFold(; init = E -> (1.0, 0.0), lift = identity,\n           combine = ((a1, b1), (a2, b2)) -> (a2 * a1, a2 * b1 + b2), observe = last);\n\njulia> accumulate(recurrence, [(0.5, 1.0), (0.5, 1.0), (0.5, 1.0)])\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 1.5\n 1.75\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.IR.GenericFold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.TracedArray","page":"Tempest","text":"An array a program is written with: a stand-in that records what is done to it as nodes of its tape. It is memory, as a Julia array is: an array the traced code allocates can be written, and views, reshapes and transposes share the memory of the array they are made from. A package that adds functions programs call defines them on it, in Tempest's primitives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.TracedArray"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.Traced","page":"Tempest","text":"A number a program computes: a stand-in for a scalar result such as sum(x), and, inside a broadcast, for an element of an array, which the broadcast's function runs on, each primitive operation it performs recorded. A package whose scalar function does not trace, a ccall say, defines it on Traced in operations that do; scalar_partials rules receive it too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Traced"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.record_map","page":"Tempest","text":"record_map(f, args...) -> Traced\n\nf applied elementwise to the operands, traced numbers and plain ones, broadcast together, recorded as one map: a primitive of the program, as tanh is, which every target runs as f on the elements. A package whose scalar function branches on its argument makes it a primitive with a method on Traced that records it so, and differentiable with a scalar_partials rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.record_map"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.contract","page":"Tempest","text":"contract(a, b, la, lb, lo)\n\nThe product of a and b summed over shared axes, as an einsum: la and lb label the axes of a and b, lo those of the result, and labels of both factors missing from the result are summed over. Every product is one: a * b of matrices is contract(a, b, (1, 2), (2, 3), (1, 3)), and so is each product of an Einops einsum.\n\njulia> Tempest.Compiler.contract([1.0 2; 3 4], [5.0 6; 7 8], (1, 2), (2, 3), (1, 3))\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 19.0  22.0\n 43.0  50.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.differentiating","page":"Tempest","text":"Tempest.Compiler.differentiating(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x, traced, is traced inside the function of a Tempest.gradient, as a layer that trains differently from how it infers asks: NNlib's within_gradient is this on traced arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.differentiating"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.scalar_partials","page":"Tempest","text":"scalar_partials(f, y, xs...) -> tuple\n\nThe partial derivatives of y = f(xs...), a function of numbers, with respect to each argument: a handle or number per argument, or nothing where the argument has none. y is the result, which a derivative such as exp's reuses; the arguments are handles, or numbers where they are constants.\n\nscalar_partials(::typeof(tanh), y, x) = (1 - y * y,)\nscalar_partials(::typeof(/), y, a, b) = (1 / b, -y / b)\n\nThe caller multiplies each partial into the cotangent of y: a condition selects it where it holds, a reciprocal 1 / x divides it by x, and a partial no gradient needs is never computed. Define a method for a function Tempest records whole, one that branches on its argument, say, to make it differentiable, or for one whose code traces, to differentiate it by the statement where it is broadcast, where the code's derivative at a branch is not the function's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.scalar_partials"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.stated_derivatives","page":"Tempest","text":"stated_derivatives(f, y, xs...) -> tuple or nothing\n\nThe partial derivatives of y = f(xs...) with respect to each argument, as f's package states them through ChainRulesCore's @scalar_rule, nothing for an argument with none; nothing for a function with no statement: Flux's xlogy, NNlib's activations and SpecialFunctions' erf state theirs. They are taken as the function's own where it has none of its own, for a function whose code traces, whose derivative at a branch may not be the function's, and for one recorded whole, whose code cannot be differentiated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.stated_derivatives"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Tempest.Compiler.stopgrad","page":"Tempest","text":"Tempest.stopgrad(x) -> x\n\nx itself, through which no gradient flows: a constant to every derivative taken of the code it is in, of any order. x is a number, an array, or a struct or tuple of them. Where x is used elsewhere, the gradient flows there. A straight-through rounding is\n\nx .+ Tempest.stopgrad(round.(x) .- x)\n\nrounded in value and the identity in derivative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.stopgrad"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#The-CUDA-target","page":"The CUDA target","text":"CUDATarget runs a program on NVIDIA GPUs; it needs CUDACore loaded. What it is made with says where the program runs, what builds its kernels, and how its sums and products may round.","title":"The CUDA target"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#Devices","page":"The CUDA target","text":"A program runs where its arrays are, whatever device the calling task is on, and a target is at home (see Placement) on the first device an argument is on, or, with every array on the host, on the calling task's device.\n\ndevice, as CUDA numbers them from 0, names the home instead: where arrays on the host go, and where claims are timed.","title":"Devices"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#Implementations","page":"The CUDA target","text":"The target uses the floor and the implementations given, as in CUDATarget(cuTileGenerator()), ties between their estimates going to the first; by default Tempest's own whose packages are loaded when it is made, cuBLAS's, cuDNN's and cuTile's (see cuBLASProducts, cuDNNAttention and cuTileGenerator).\n\nAnother package's implementation takes part only when it is given, and with none at all, CUDATarget(precision, online, ()), the floor runs every kernel.","title":"Implementations"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#Precision-of-products","page":"The CUDA target","text":"precision is the numerics policy for products:\n\nprecision Float32 products\n:full, the default kept in Float32\n:tf32 may use TF32 tensor cores\n:tf32x3 each Float32 factor split into two TF32 parts, three tensor-core products summed (close to Float32 accuracy)\n\nHalf-precision products always accumulate in Float32, and a product of a half-precision value, read through its conversion to Float32, and a Float32 one is a half-precision product under any precision, the Float32 factor rounded to the half type: a BFloat16 attention's backward rounds its probabilities and their gradient so, as FlashAttention and cuDNN do, where the CPU multiplies in Float32 as the program does. The target trades that precision for speed as cu does, which makes arrays Float32.","title":"Precision of products"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#The-online-softmax","page":"The CUDA target","text":"online permits the online softmax (see rewrite): a sum weighted by a softmax accumulates in one pass with the softmax's maximum, rescaled as it grows, which changes rounding.","title":"The online softmax"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#A-backward-that-reads-rounded-sums","page":"The CUDA target","text":"rounded_backward lets a backward read a forward's sum as the program rounds it to half precision where the sums of the derivative are exchanged (see exchanged_sums): the softmax backward's Σ dO·o reads the output o as a half-precision forward stores it, as FlashAttention and cuDNN read theirs, rather than in Float32, and the forward stores the rounded output only.\n\nWith it false the backward reads the sum as the program computes it: in BFloat16 at 64 features, 8 heads, 1024 positions, the gradients' largest error is 6.2e-3 of their largest element rather than 6.8e-3 for a loss of the output's squares, where Σ dO·o nearly cancels the sums it is taken from, and 5.5e-3 either way when the output's cotangent comes from elsewhere.","title":"A backward that reads rounded sums"},{"category":"section","location":"cuda/#Deterministic-sums","page":"The CUDA target","text":"deterministic keeps a sum's rounding from depending on the order its blocks run in. Without it a sweep may compute a sum in the sweep's orientation and add it into its value atomically, as FlashAttention-2's backward adds the queries' gradient, which then differs from run to run in its last bits: attention's training step in Float32 at 1024 positions, 64 features, 8 heads, causal and TF32 takes 437 μs, and 702 deterministic.\n\nA sum by key, as an embedding's gradient is, accumulates atomically either way.","title":"Deterministic sums"},{"category":"section","location":"work/#A-launch's-work","page":"A launch's work","text":"Work is one launch's work, as an implementation states it for Tempest to price.","title":"A launch's work"},{"category":"section","location":"work/#What-a-launch-states","page":"A launch's work","text":"field what it states\nbytes bytes moved\nreread bytes it reads again (what its blocks read of an argument beyond the first time)\npassed bytes it passes to or takes from another of its claim's launches (partial results in a workspace, as a split sum's parts)\nflops, factor flops of products whose factors are of type factor\nrecomputed flops of folds it recomputes inside them\nblocks, split its blocks, and whether they split the sums among them\napplied the scalar functions it applies elementwise, each with the number of positions it is applied at\nswept functions applied inside a product's sweep, to its factors\ninner functions applied inside a fold the sweep recomputes, to that fold's tiles\nsteps the steps a block takes, where blocks sweep tiles one after another\nstep_runs what a block loads at a step: of each load, the bytes it reads in one run, contiguous, paired with the bytes it reads in the step\nstepped the functions the steps compute on what they loaded, each with the number of steps it is applied in along their chain\nrefold_steps the steps apart in which a block recomputes a fold whose body is not bilinear along its axis","title":"What a launch states"},{"category":"section","location":"work/#How-it-is-priced","page":"A launch's work","text":"Tempest prices the bytes on its package's memory, those read again from the cache, those passed within the cache where they fit it, as the launch that takes them finds them there, the flops on the line its package reaches for products of that class, and each function on the time it measured for it on the device (see seconds).\n\nFunctions applied inside a product's sweep, to its factors, are swept: each step's small tile of them stalls the sweep, and they cost many times what they cost streamed; those applied inside a fold the sweep recomputes, to that fold's tiles, are inner, at a factor of their own.","title":"How it is priced"},{"category":"section","location":"work/#Sweeps-in-steps","page":"A launch's work","text":"A launch whose blocks sweep tiles one after another, waiting for what each step loads before the next, states the steps a block takes, what a block loads at a step (step_runs: of each load, the bytes it reads in one run, contiguous, paired with the bytes it reads in the step), and the functions the steps compute on what they loaded, each with the number of steps it is applied in along their chain (stepped): a step takes its latency and its bytes at what one block reaches, however few positions it covers, and a sweep of many narrow steps takes that many times as long, whatever bytes it moves.\n\nTempest prices a step on its package's memory and each function on the time it measured for it in a step (see seconds). A launch that states no steps, or one, is priced by its bytes, flops and functions alone, as a product's sweep is by its line.","title":"Sweeps in steps"},{"category":"section","location":"work/#Folds-recomputed","page":"A launch's work","text":"A fold a launch recomputes where it reads it, its flops recomputed, a block sweeps in steps of its own, before the sweep or inside each step the fold moves along: a product's steps, among the sweep's, where its body is bilinear along its axis, and otherwise refold_steps apart, combining its body elementwise, which wait on their own loads one after another with the rest of the block's work, however the sweep hides its bytes; Tempest prices each as a step of the sweep.","title":"Folds recomputed"},{"category":"section","location":"programs/#Programs","page":"Programs","text":"A program is Julia array code, and what it computes reaches the compiler as three things: maps, folds (a scan is a fold that keeps the axis it runs along, a scatter one that combines each element at a position it computes), and reads at index formulas. Products, reshapes, permutations, gathers and scatters are spellings of them, and so is every function a program calls.","title":"Programs"},{"category":"section","location":"programs/#What-Tempest-names","page":"Programs","text":"Tempest names only what Base cannot spell:\n\nthe way in: compile, explain, gradient and withgradient, the targets, and the implementations of kernel packages Tempest has extensions for;\nthe derivative of a scalar function, which its code does not carry: scalar_partials, and a value no gradient flows through: stopgrad;\nthe laws of a fold's combine: GenericFold.","title":"What Tempest names"},{"category":"section","location":"programs/#Folds-and-scans","page":"Programs","text":"A fold is Base's mapreduce, its state a tuple if need be, and a scan Base's accumulate; a fold's derivative comes from its combine's, through scans.\n\nA function given to mapreduce is taken to associate and commute, as a reduction groups and interleaves its elements; one given to accumulate is combined in sequence, as Base combines it, from an init that need be no identity.\n\nA GenericFold, given to reduce or accumulate where the function goes, claims that it associates and commutes, and so a scan by it may group its elements, as a sum's may.","title":"Folds and scans"},{"category":"section","location":"programs/#logsumexp","page":"Programs","text":"logsumexp is a fold, a spelling. The log-sum-exp written out with maximum differentiates as it does, and plans as it does where the target permits the rewrites of sums (see Compiler.rewrite); it is named still because where a target keeps the rounding as written, the spelling is two passes, the maximum and then the sum, and logsumexp one.","title":"logsumexp"},{"category":"section","location":"programs/#Other-functions","page":"Programs","text":"Any other function, a product by labelled axes or a batched product say, is a spelling and lives in its own package, as Einops's and NNlib's do; an extension gives it a method on traced arrays where its body does not trace.","title":"Other functions"},{"category":"section","location":"targets/#Adding-a-target","page":"Adding a target","text":"A target is where a program runs. A package that adds one defines the names below for its type, as CUDAExt does for CUDATarget; the CPU's definitions are Tempest's own.","title":"Adding a target"},{"category":"type","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.Target","page":"Adding a target","text":"Where a program runs: CPUTarget, CUDATarget, or a type a package adds, defining the protocol of this file for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Target"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.target_for","page":"Adding a target","text":"target_for(x) -> target or nothing\n\nThe target an argument selects: CPUTarget() for an Array, a package's target for its arrays, on the device that holds them, nothing for anything else. A program is prepared on the first target among its inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.target_for"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.device_of","page":"Adding a target","text":"device_of(x) -> Int or nothing\n\nThe device whose memory holds x, an array or a target's storage, by the number its package gives it; nothing for the host's memory, the CPU's. A target reads arrays on the host, copying them where they cannot be its storage, and the CPU reads a device's arrays so. A program whose arrays are on several devices is planned with a device for each value and a copy where a value is read on another (see plans_across_devices); on a target that does not plan so, its arrays and the target are on one device at most, beside the host, and a call that brings arrays of another device than its target's throws (see point!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.device_of"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.default_device","page":"Adding a target","text":"default_device(target) -> Int or nothing\n\nWhere a target that names no device is at home when no argument is on one: the host's memory for the CPU, the calling task's device for CUDA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.default_device"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.at_home","page":"Adding a target","text":"at_home(target, device) -> target\n\ntarget at home on device: what a preparation builds and times with, once its arguments have said where home is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.at_home"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.on_device","page":"Adding a target","text":"on_device(f, target, device = home_device(target))\n\nf() with the calling task on device of target's kind, its state on the device it was on (for CUDA, its context and stream) as it was when f returns or throws: what touches a device, building, binding and running a program, runs on its own, whatever device the caller is on. An executable's steps are put on theirs by walk alone. A target of no devices, as the CPU, runs f() as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.on_device"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.implementations","page":"Adding a target","text":"The implementations a target uses beside the floor, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.implementations"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.numerics","page":"Adding a target","text":"The target's numerics, as part of the tuning record's key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.numerics"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.permits_rewrites","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether a target permits the rewrites of sums that change rounding (see rewrite).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.permits_rewrites"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.rounded_backward","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether a backward reads a forward's sum as the program rounds it (see CUDATarget).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.rounded_backward"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.deterministic","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether a target keeps a sum's rounding from depending on the order its blocks run in (see CUDATarget).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.deterministic"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.product_class","page":"Adding a target","text":"The class of products of T factors: a half type by name, Float32 by the target's precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.product_class"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.allocate","page":"Adding a target","text":"allocate(target, T, n) -> buffer\n\nA buffer of n elements of T on target, dense and one-dimensional: the storage of a program's values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.allocate"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.upload","page":"Adding a target","text":"upload(target, x) -> buffer\n\nThe elements of the array x as a buffer on target: how a program's constants get there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.upload"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.upload!","page":"Adding a target","text":"upload!(target, buffer, x) -> buffer\n\nCopy the array x, which is not storage on target, into buffer, which is: how a caller's array that cannot be an argument's storage gets into the buffer that is (see bind! and point!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.upload!"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.download!","page":"Adding a target","text":"download!(target, x, buffer) -> x\n\nCopy buffer, storage on target, into the array x, which is not: how what a program wrote into an argument gets back into a caller's array that could not be its storage (see point!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.download!"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.bindable","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether array x can be storage on target, bound by reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.bindable"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.pointable","page":"Adding a target","text":"pointable(target, x, at) -> Bool\n\nWhether launches built on storage target allocated can read array x, whose first element is at address at, where it is instead (see repoint!): x is storage the target could have allocated, as aligned as its kernels assume of what it allocates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.pointable"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.task_state","page":"Adding a target","text":"task_state(target) -> function\n\nA function that, called first in a new task, gives the task the state on target of the task that asked for it (for CUDA, its context and stream), and none where that task has none: a preparation that builds nothing asks implementations side by side what they offer (see side_by_side), and touches no device, as while a package precompiles without one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.task_state"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.floor_launch","page":"Adding a target","text":"floor_launch(target, kernel::ScalarKernel, args) -> launch\n\nA launch of a floor kernel on target: launch() computes every item of kernel over args, the buffers of its slots and workspaces as a tuple. The CPU runs the items in order, in a function generated from the kernel's body; a device runs them at once, one thread per item, so a scatter's items add with accumulate!. A kernel with a split runs each lane of each part of each item, in the names its body reads: lin the item, part of parts, and lane of lanes in the part; the CPU runs a part as one lane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.floor_launch"},{"category":"type","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.ScalarKernel","page":"Adding a target","text":"A kernel of the floor: body computes item lin, zero-based, of items, over the buffers of the values slots, dense and one-dimensional, and then those of its workspaces, which the function generated from the body receives as a tuple in that order (see floor_launch). A kernel with a split computes one lane of a part of each item.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.ScalarKernel"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.accumulate!","page":"Adding a target","text":"accumulate!(fold, buffer, i, x)\n\nCombine element x into state i of a fold by key, in buffer: in place on the CPU, where a floor kernel's items run in order, and atomically on a device (see CUDAExt), where they run at once and the elements a position takes combine in no fixed order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.accumulate!"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.floor_split","page":"Adding a target","text":"floor_split(target, items, positions, adjacent) -> Split or nothing\n\nHow the floor folds positions positions for each of items items on target, neighbouring items neighbours in memory when adjacent: nothing for one lane per item, which folds them in order, as on the CPU; or a Split, which deals them among lanes. A target that splits runs a part's lanes together and merges their states with merge_lanes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.floor_split"},{"category":"type","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.Split","page":"Adding a target","text":"Split(parts, lanes, side = 1)\n\nHow the floor deals the positions each item folds among lanes, where items alone would leave a device idle: parts parts of lanes lanes each, as the target asks for it (see floor_split). Each lane folds every parts * lanesth position from its own, the lanes of a part merge their states (merge_lanes), and a part keeps its state in a workspace, which the next launch merges with the item's other parts'. So a split group is a launch per phase of its folds, and one after them that stores them and sweeps what reads them, its positions dealt among the lanes as the folds' were. On CUDA a part is a block and its lanes threads, the same part of side neighbouring items in one block, side by side; on the CPU a part has one lane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Split"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.merge_lanes","page":"Adding a target","text":"merge_lanes(fold, state, workspace) -> state\n\nThe merge of state over the lanes of a part of a split item (see Split), which each lane gets. The fold's workspace names the target, as the buffer of accumulate! does: on the CPU, where a part has one lane, the merge is state itself; on a device the lanes merge their states (see CUDAExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.merge_lanes"},{"category":"type","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.Step","page":"Adding a target","text":"Step(run, device, from = device)\n\nA step of an execution: run(), a kernel's launch or a copy, and the device it runs on (see device_of); and the device it reads from, another only for a crossing, a copy from from to device (see crossing). The two name the stream it runs on: a device's own (see stream), or the lane of the ordered pair of devices a crossing copies between (see lane).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Step"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.crosses","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether a group of the kind reads on another device than the one it writes on: a crossing's, which copies a value from the device it is stored on. A group of any other kind reads and writes on one.\n\n\n\n\n\ncrosses(step) -> Bool\n\nWhether step copies between two devices, a crossing's (see crossing), on the lane of the two rather than on a device's own stream: a target with graphs says how a run of crossings is recorded (see capture).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.crosses"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.taken","page":"Adding a target","text":"The kernel formed there from seed, where forming it here, through context cx, would read nothing that differs.\n\n\n\n\n\ntaken(target, x)\n\nTake x, an array a call shares with its caller, a result or an argument bound by reference, for the launches the call is about to make, with the task on the array's device: the launches read and write it by its address, unseen by the package whose array it is. What other streams still have to run on x is waited for, and whatever reads it after the call, on any stream or task, waits for the launches. A target whose arrays' package orders an array's uses by the stream each was submitted on takes the array's address here, which that package counts as the calling task's use of it from then on, until another stream's. The CPU's run in order, and it does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.taken"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.crossing","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether value v of graph is read across devices: a crossing's copy.\n\n\n\n\n\ncrossing(target, to, from, values, offset) -> run\n\nA step's run that copies into buffer to, storage on another of target's devices, as many elements of buffer from, storage on one, as to holds, those after the first offset: how a value read on another device than its own gets there (see Crossed), all of it at offset 0, or a span of it, which is all its readers there read (see storage_span). values names the two buffers, to_value => from_value, for the run to be pointed elsewhere as a Copy is (see repoint!); the offset is the run's own, where in the source value's storage the span starts, kept as either buffer is replaced. It is built with the task on to's device, and runs where its lane puts the task (see on_stream); each buffer knows its own device. Its schedule orders it after what wrote its source and before what reads its copy (see Schedule). A target with graphs says, as it records a run of crossings (see capture), whether the crossing is recorded or runs as the run is replayed. On the CPU, whose devices are labels on the host's memory, it is a copy on the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.crossing"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.stream","page":"Adding a target","text":"stream(target, device) -> stream\n\nWhat a device's own steps run on (see stream_of), its kernels and copies in order. Made once for each schedule, with the task on the device. By default the pair (device, device), whose steps run with the task on the device (see on_stream).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.stream"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.lane","page":"Adding a target","text":"lane(target, from, to, out) -> stream\n\nWhat the crossings from device from to device to run on (see stream_of), in order: the lane of the ordered pair, which leaves from by out, its link (see link). Made once for each schedule, with the task on to's device. By default the pair (from, to), whose steps run with the task on to (see on_stream).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.lane"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.link","page":"Adding a target","text":"link(target, device) -> link\n\nWhat the crossings leaving device go out by, every lane from it the same (see lane): it takes them one at a time, in the order they are launched, which is the order their sources are written (see walk and run_order), so that none waits behind one whose source is not. Made once for each schedule, for each device a crossing leaves, with the task on it. By default nothing: the CPU's crossings are copies on the host, which run as they are launched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.link"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.on_stream","page":"Adding a target","text":"on_stream(f, target, stream)\n\nf() with the calling task where the steps of stream run, as on_device puts it on a device. By default on the stream's last device: a device's own, or the reader's of a lane, where a crossing's run is built and expects to run (see crossing). A target whose lanes are streams of their own enters them here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.on_stream"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.task_stream","page":"Adding a target","text":"task_stream(target) -> stream\n\nThe stream the calling task's launches run on, on the device the task is on (see on_device): what an executable keeps of its last use on each device, and compares with the next (see taken!). On a target whose steps have run as they return, the CPU's, nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.task_stream"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.event","page":"Adding a target","text":"event(target, stream) -> event\n\nWhat a step on stream records once it has run, for steps on other streams to wait for (see record! and wait!), made with the task where the stream's steps run. A target whose steps run in the order they are launched, the CPU's, has none: nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.event"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.record!","page":"Adding a target","text":"record!(target, stream, event)\n\nRecord event on stream, after what was launched on it so far, with the task where the stream's steps run (see on_stream): after a step that a step on another stream waits for, and as a call begins and ends (see walk). Recorded into a graph (see capture), each launch of the graph records it again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.record!"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.wait!","page":"Adding a target","text":"wait!(target, stream, event)\n\nMake what is launched on stream from now on wait for what was launched before the last record! of event, with the task where the stream's steps run (see on_stream): before a step that waits for a step on another stream, and as a call begins and ends (see walk). Recorded into a graph, the wait is for the last record launched before the graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.wait!"},{"category":"type","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.Scheduled","page":"Adding a target","text":"Scheduled(step, stream, waits, signal)\n\nA step as its schedule runs it (see Schedule): on stream (see stream), after waiting for waits, the events of steps on other streams, and recording signal once it has run, where a step on another stream waits for it, else nothing. What a target captures and replays (see capture).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.Scheduled"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.capture","page":"Adding a target","text":"capture(target, steps, keep) -> graph\n\nsteps, each a Scheduled step that launches work on target, all on one stream (a run of a Schedule), captured in order, their waits and records with them, as one graph for replay(target, graph) to launch again; retarget! changes the addresses one step's work reads. Tempest asks with the task where the stream's steps run (see on_stream), where the graph is made, launched and changed. The graph keeps keep, what it launches, alive until release(target, graph) or the collector destroys it. A target without graphs replays the steps themselves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.capture"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.replay","page":"Adding a target","text":"Launch graph, what capture recorded on target.\n\n\n\n\n\nreplay(d, r) -> Decided or nothing\n\nDecisions r replayed on decider d's graph: the plan of the proposals taken, planned once, each group's text checked against the one kept, and each recurrence's step replayed from those kept with them. Nothing where a group differs or a step was not kept: the cover reaches them instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.replay"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.release","page":"Adding a target","text":"Destroy graph, what capture recorded on target, once nothing replays it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.release"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.recapture","page":"Adding a target","text":"recapture(target, graph, steps, keep) -> graph\n\ngraph, which capture recorded from steps, recorded again after their launches were pointed at other storage (see repoint!). A target with graphs may update the graph it has rather than make another.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.recapture"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.retarget!","page":"Adding a target","text":"retarget!(target, graph, steps, moves, repoint) -> Bool\n\nMake what graph recorded of a launch, steps among the steps capture took, read where moves say its values moved, each a named tuple of the value, its old and new addresses and its bytes, (value, from, to, bytes). repoint() points the launch at the new storage (see repoint!), and is called once, by the target or after it: a target may record the launch alone before and after, to learn where its recording holds an address. False when the graph cannot be changed so, and must be recorded again (recapture). The steps themselves, a target's graph without graphs, read where their launches read: true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.retarget!"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.device_name","page":"Adding a target","text":"device_name(target[, device])\n\nThe name of the device target runs on, which names its tuning record and its rates file; or of another of its devices, as device_of numbers them, which a program placed over several runs on (see Placement). A target's devices are of one kind unless it says otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.device_name"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.toolchain","page":"Adding a target","text":"toolchain(target) -> Vector{Pair{String, String}}\n\nWhat builds the kernels target runs, each part by name and version, which names its tuning record and its rates file with its device: what they hold are times of kernels built so, and a kernel another toolchain builds is another kernel to time. Nothing for a target that builds none, as the CPU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.toolchain"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.measurable","page":"Adding a target","text":"Whether claims on target can be timed at preparation; time_launch times one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.measurable"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.time_launch","page":"Adding a target","text":"time_launch(target, launch) -> seconds::Float64\n\nThe seconds one call of launch() takes on the device among the launches of a captured graph, its data in memory rather than in the cache, as the cover times claims: a step reads its weights once, and finds them in memory. Only a measurable target defines it. Tempest calls it one timing at a time, once the device's work is done, so what it keeps between timings needs no lock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.time_launch"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.time_crossing","page":"Adding a target","text":"time_crossing(target, from, to, bytes) -> seconds::Float64\n\nThe seconds a crossing of bytes from device from to device to of target takes (see crossing), run alone on the lane of the two (see lane), from when the lane may start to when what reads the copy may: what a decider fits the link between them on (see link!). Only a measurable target that plans across devices defines it. Tempest calls it one timing at a time, once both devices' work is done.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.time_crossing"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.quiesce","page":"Adding a target","text":"quiesce(target, stream)\n\nWait until stream, a task's as task_stream gave it, has run everything that task launched on it, with the calling task on the stream's device: a task that uses an executable another stream used last waits so (see taken!). The CPU's steps have run as they return, and it does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\nquiesce(target)\n\nWait until the device has run everything launched on it, by any task. Every timing waits so before it times a claim (see timed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.quiesce"},{"category":"function","location":"targets/#Tempest.Compiler.device_rates","page":"Adding a target","text":"device_rates(target) -> rates\n\nThe device's rates as it reports them (see Rates): a named tuple of launch, the seconds between launches in a captured graph; cached and bandwidth, bytes per second for a launch that moves what the cache holds and for one that moves more, each from memory; cache, its bytes; and blocks, the blocks that fill the device. They are not measured: off by twice or half, they change no plan of the corpus's, and the rates that do, each package's products and each scalar function's seconds, are fitted on first need. Only a measurable target defines it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.device_rates"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tempest.jl","page":"Home","text":"Tempest compiles Julia array programs into fused GPU kernels. A whole training step, with the loss, its gradient and the optimizer, is traced once from plain Julia, planned into kernels, and replayed as one CUDA graph. Kernel packages such as cuTile and cuBLAS compete to run each part, and whatever no package claims still runs.\n\nusing Tempest, CUDACore, cuTile, cuBLAS\n\nstruct Dense{W, B}\n    w::W\n    b::B\nend\n(d::Dense)(x) = tanh.(d.w * x .+ d.b)\nloss(m, x) = sum(abs2, m(x))\n\nm = Dense(CuArray(randn(Float32, 64, 32)), CuArray(randn(Float32, 64)))\nx = CuArray(randn(Float32, 32, 16))\n\ngrad = compile((m, x) -> Tempest.withgradient(loss, m, x), m, x)\nl, (dm, dx) = grad(m, x)   # dm is a named tuple (w = ..., b = ...)\nexplain(grad)            # each kernel: what it computes and stores, and what runs it\n\nWithout a GPU, the same code runs on the CPU with plain arrays.","title":"Tempest.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Loading","page":"Home","text":"Tempest and its extensions compile their own code into package images as they precompile, so that a session compiles little of it again. Julia keeps ten images of a package (JULIA_MAX_NUM_PRECOMPILE_FILES), and each environment, each checkout of Tempest and each set of flags such as --check-bounds needs its own: past ten, the image evicted is compiled again, a minute or more, at its next using, so raise the cap where you keep several. precompile_workload = false under [Tempest] in an environment's LocalPreferences.toml skips the work Tempest and its extensions do while they precompile, for an environment whose Tempest changes often, at the cost of compiling that code in each session.","title":"Loading"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-holds","page":"Home","text":"Tempest owns coverage and kernel packages own speed. A program written with Base's arrays and the primitives on the Tempest page runs with no kernel package loaded, on the kernels Tempest generates for itself, so loading a package or removing one changes how fast a program runs and never whether it runs. Each kind of change has one place: a new function is array code in the package that owns it, a new kernel is a change to a kernel package, and a better optimization is a change to Tempest.\n\nWhat else holds is stated by the tests, not here. A property not yet met is a broken test under a name that says what would close it, and TRIALS.md in the repository is the docket of what the code is being measured against.","title":"What holds"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Programs says what a program is to the compiler, Compiling and calling what a compiled function reads, keeps and returns and how its plan is measured, and The CUDA target where a program runs and how its products round. The Tempest page documents the functions Tempest exports for compiling, differentiating and explaining a program, and the primitives programs can call.\n\nThe pages under Tempest.Compiler are for packages beside a program: preparing a program and the steps beneath compile, the invariants a plan keeps, what a package that adds a target defines, what kernel packages build kernels with, the vocabulary they read, the interface they implement and how a launch's work is stated and priced.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#Plan-invariants","page":"Plan invariants","text":"verify checks the invariants the kernels of a plan rely on, and throws an InvalidPlan naming the value and the kernel at the first that fails. The exception carries the invariant's number here.\n\nprepare checks every plan it forms, and its storage, with verify = true, the default when the environment sets TEMPEST_VERIFY=true, as the tests do.","title":"Plan invariants"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#1.-Owners-and-order","page":"Plan invariants","text":"Every value a kernel stores is a member of that kernel's group and of no other; every value a kernel or a result reads is an input, a constant, or stored by a kernel earlier in the order.","title":"1. Owners and order"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#2.-Storage","page":"Plan invariants","text":"With storage, simulating the order:\n\nno slot is given to a value while a kernel that uses the value it holds does not run before the value's, by the order the program gives the kernels (see plan_order: on one stream earlier, or before it through the values it reads), but where a pair the storage records allows it, the kernel the last to use it;\neach donated, staged, overwritten and reused pair holds by the rule that allows it, tested again, the other kernels reading what it writes over running before its kernel by that order;\nand each lifetime covers its value's kernel and every reader.\n\nOn one device, whose kernels are one stream, a value's users run before a kernel where they are earlier.\n\nin_place(k) says whether kernel k's realization reads each element a set of its outputs needs before it writes any of them, as storage was told.","title":"2. Storage"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#3.-Recurrences","page":"Plan invariants","text":"A recurrence's group has every state of its cycle first among its members, each carried from a different value it computes, and stores the values of the cycle no other kernel stores; each member outside the cycle rides its steps along them or at their last, and each fold of it is a sum that rides them, through values nothing stores.","title":"3. Recurrences"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#4.-Terms","page":"Plan invariants","text":"Each member's term loads values stored apart, its own only in a recurrence's; each member has a place within the domain; a fold read as a Stat is a primary fold of the group complete in an earlier phase; and a fold a term recomputes is not a member of the group.","title":"4. Terms"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#5.-Devices","page":"Plan invariants","text":"Every read joins values on one device but a crossing's, which reads a value on another device than its own, one span of its storage (see storage_span); every member of a kernel, and every value it reads, is on its device, but a crossing's kernel's, whose one member is a crossing, reading its source (both stored, by 1); with storage, a slot holds values of one device, and a result that replaces an argument is on that argument's device.","title":"5. Devices"},{"category":"section","location":"invariants/#6.-Extents","page":"Plan invariants","text":"Every kernel's domain has positions: no axis of it is of extent zero. A value of no elements is a constant, the one array of its shape and type, and a fold over no elements is what it starts from, so that no implementation is asked for a kernel over an axis of none.","title":"6. Extents"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Preparing-a-program","page":"Preparing a program","text":"prepare(program, inputs...) plans program for the shapes and types of inputs and builds its kernels on target, chosen from the inputs' array types by default. It is the level beneath compile, for a step run in place.","title":"Preparing a program"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#A-training-loop","page":"Preparing a program","text":"A training loop, where the step returns the loss and the new parameters and optimizer state:\n\nex = prepare(trace(step, params, state, batch), params, state, batch; replaces = (2 => 1, 3 => 2))\ncapture!(ex)\nloss = first(outputs(ex))       # where each step leaves the loss\nfor batch in batches\n    bind!(ex, 3 => batch)       # only the batch is new\n    replay!(ex)                 # parameters and state are updated where they are\n    @show loss[]\nend\n\nA function that writes its arguments needs none of this: compile writes them where the caller has them, for any arrays a call brings.","title":"A training loop"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Measuring","page":"Preparing a program","text":"Close calls between claims are timed when measure asks and the target can, and kept in the tuning record.\n\nmeasure what is timed\n:none, the default no claim, but the times in the record are still used\n:close the options whose estimates are within the close-call factor of the best\n:all every option of every group and both sides of every comparison\n\ntrue and false mean :close and :none. The environment's TEMPEST_MEASURE (none, close or all) overrides the default, and measure given overrides the environment. On a device other jobs share, a close call timed is decided by chance.","title":"Measuring"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Decisions-kept-and-replayed","page":"Preparing a program","text":"What the cover decided, the proposals it took and the claim that runs each kernel, is kept beside the tuning record (see Tempest.Compiler.reach!), and a preparation of the same program, on the same target and device, with the record and the rates file holding what those decisions read, replays it: it plans the program once, and plans no trial, prices and measures nothing.\n\nAnything else a decision reads that differs, the program's extents, element types or captured constants, the target's numerics, Tempest's own code or a time recorded since, makes them the cover's to reach again.\n\nDecisions are replayed only where the cover would reach them again on the record as it is: a preparation that measured as it decided covers again on what it measured, until a cover measures nothing, three covers at most, and keeps the last one's; with the environment's TEMPEST_SETTLE=false it keeps the first's, which the next preparation reaches again.\n\nWith TEMPEST_REPLAY=false a preparation neither replays decisions nor keeps its own: the cover reaches them every time. A fresh TEMPEST_CACHE holds no decisions, no record and no kernels.","title":"Decisions kept and replayed"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#A-claim-that-fails-to-build","page":"Preparing a program","text":"A claim that fails to build is no option once it has: the program is planned again without it, each map of the group it claimed proposed to be stored apart, so that a scalar function a kernel package cannot lower runs on the floor and the rest of the group on the package. explain names the claims that failed.","title":"A claim that fails to build"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#State","page":"Preparing a program","text":"replaces declares state, as pairs result => argument of positions among the program's results and its arguments, alike in structure, shapes and types: (2 => 1, 3 => 2) for a step (params, state, batch) -> (loss, params, state). After each execution the argument holds the result, as if bound again, so the next execution reads it where it is.\n\nAn input the program writes is replaced so by what it writes; a result that is the input as it was before the write is copied apart before the kernels run.\n\nA result is written into the argument's storage when the kernel that writes it reads the argument only at the address it writes, and the kernels that read the argument otherwise can run first; else it is written apart and copied there after the last kernel. explain says which.","title":"State"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Where-the-inputs-live","page":"Preparing a program","text":"bind! copies inputs into storage the executable owns, except the arrays at the positions in by_reference, which must be of the target's array type: they are the executable's storage. The caller keeps them alive and may write them between executions, and a result that replaces one writes into it.\n\npoint! makes the caller's arrays the storage at each binding instead, as compile does.\n\nThe preparation binds inputs as bind! does, or as point! does with point = true, as compile prepares: the kernels are built on the caller's arrays where they can read them there, and the executable makes no storage of its own for those arguments until a binding copies into it, holding the arrays as point! does, until it is bound to others.","title":"Where the inputs live"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Pinning-what-the-cover-decides","page":"Preparing a program","text":"recompute pins folds to be recomputed where they are read rather than stored, by their numbers among the values of the program's index graph, as explain prints them: what the cover would otherwise decide by cost (see Tempest.Compiler.recomputable_folds).\n\nride pins values computed from a recurrence's steps to be computed as the steps run, so numbered: a sum over the steps added at each, a value along them written at each, one of the last step written at it; what the cover would otherwise decide by cost (see Tempest.Compiler.riding_values).\n\npartition decides which of the forward's maps the backward recomputes rather than reads, by a minimum cut of what saving them costs (see Tempest.Compiler.partition); false saves every map the backward reads, as a trial's comparison.","title":"Pinning what the cover decides"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Planning-without-building","page":"Preparing a program","text":"build = false plans and decides but builds and allocates nothing: the executable can be explained, not run, on a machine without the target's device. No claim is timed, and claims are priced on nominal rates.","title":"Planning without building"},{"category":"section","location":"preparing/#Verifying","page":"Preparing a program","text":"verify = true checks every plan the preparation forms, the cover's trials among them, and the storage of the one it runs, throwing where one breaks an invariant the kernels rely on (see Plan invariants). It is the default where the environment sets TEMPEST_VERIFY=true, as the tests do.\n\nWhere it sets TEMPEST_CROSSCHECK=true, as the tests do too, decisions a preparation replays are checked against those its cover reaches, which it reaches as well, at a cover's cost.","title":"Verifying"},{"category":"section","location":"kernels/#Building-kernels","page":"Building kernels","text":"What kernel packages build kernels with: the kernel cache, generated and concurrently; and in_own_store, which their precompile workloads prepare in.","title":"Building kernels"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.content_key","page":"Building kernels","text":"content_key(parts...; checked = false) -> String\n\nA hash of parts that is the same in every session for the same content: of their canonical_text. With checked, Tempest's own code is named without its identity, for a kernel stored with what its compilation used of that code (dependencies).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.content_key"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.cached_kernel","page":"Building kernels","text":"cached_kernel(compile, key) -> (entry, binary)\n\nThe kernel under key: from the object cache, or compiled by compile(), which returns (entry, binary) and stores it, when the cache has none. This happens once per key in a session, so that callers in flight together wait for the first, and a failure fails every caller. The kernel is loaded or compiled on a thread from the moment it is asked for, as neither makes a device object: the tasks that build a stage's options side by side (concurrently) take turns on one thread, and a compilation they ran there would begin only once the last of them had asked for its kernel, as the GPT step's first stage of 147 kernels asked for its last 2.8 s in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.cached_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.store_kernel","page":"Building kernels","text":"store_kernel(key, entry, binary; dependencies) -> (entry, binary)\n\nCount a compiled kernel and keep it under key, with what its compilation used (see dependencies); not where that could not be read (nothing). A failure to write only loses the entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.store_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.store_failure","page":"Building kernels","text":"store_failure(key, message; dependencies)\n\nCount a kernel that failed to compile and keep the compiler's message under key, with what its inference used, so that it fails again while that holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.store_failure"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.dependencies","page":"Building kernels","text":"dependencies(code, lookup, table) -> Vector{Dependency} or nothing\n\nWhat a kernel compiled from the code instance code used, as inference's edges record it: each call its inference made, from the kernel down, with the methods it matched in table, the method table the compilation dispatched in; each invoke; each global its edges name or a method it reached names in its code; and each type its methods were specialized on that no package's image holds. Nothing where a method's code to read its globals from is not kept: the kernel is not stored. lookup(mi) gives the code instance of a method instance an edge names, as the compiler that inferred them caches it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.dependencies"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.holds_as_stored","page":"Building kernels","text":"holds_as_stored(dependencies) -> Bool\n\nWhether each of a kernel's dependencies (see dependencies) holds as loading the kernel from the store checks it: written as the store keeps it, read back and checked (read_holds), without the store; false for a kernel not stored, whose dependencies are nothing. Each is checked, those after one that does not hold too. A kernel package's precompile workload checks its kernels' so, so that the code reading and checking the records of each kind its kernels reach is in its image, whatever one of them answers, where a session's tasks loading kernels side by side each compiled it at once. A kind no workload kernel reaches, an invoke among them, compiles in the session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.holds_as_stored"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.uncached_kernel","page":"Building kernels","text":"uncached_kernel(entry, binary) -> (entry, binary)\n\nCount a kernel compiled in this session that the cache cannot keep, one that refers to values of the session, and return it as cached_kernel expects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.uncached_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.generated","page":"Building kernels","text":"generated(source::Expr, into = Generated) -> function\n\nDefine the function source, an anonymous function expression, in module into, keeping its source: in a content_key a generated function is its source, since its name differs between sessions. A package's precompile workload defines its kernels in a module of its own, since Generated is closed to it then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.generated"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.concurrently","page":"Building kernels","text":"concurrently(f, xs, target) -> results\n\nf of each of xs, in tasks that take turns on the calling thread, each in the calling task's state on target. Groups and kernels are built this way: while one task waits for its kernel, compiling on a thread of its own (see cached_kernel), the others ask for theirs. Device objects are made on the calling thread, so at exit they are finalized after what the caller made from them later (a module unloaded before a captured graph that launches it crashes the driver). It returns once every task has ended (see ended_all).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.concurrently"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.same_storage","page":"Building kernels","text":"same_storage(a, b)\n\nWhether buffers a and b are one storage: the same elements at the same address, whichever array holds them. Values over one argument's storage each hold a vector of the caller's array, made apiece (see share!), so that two of them are one storage and never one object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.same_storage"},{"category":"type","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.SessionTable","page":"Building kernels","text":"SessionTable{K, V}()\n\nA table of answers kept for the session, read and filled under its own lock: how the compiler and the packages that implement its targets keep what they find once a session. A table is one only if its key holds everything its answer was derived from that can differ between two questions in one session, so that emptying it (forget_session!) costs time and never a wrong plan; any other table of theirs is named, with why, in NOT_SESSION_TABLES. A key holds, of what can differ:\n\ncode, named as the stores' content keys name it (definitions): a package's by the function itself, since no code changes within a session (below); any other, a function of Main or a closure over one, by its definition, which holds the values of the globals it reads, which change without a definition;\nthe device, by what the answer read of it: its architecture, its context;\nthe environment's settings the answer read, and what the preparation that asked was asked, as whether it compiles (Implement.compiling);\ncontent, as a kernel's, by its content key.\n\nAnswers are their session's: a table filled in another process, as a package's precompile workload fills its tables in the package's image, compiled on another machine perhaps and compiling nothing, holds none in the session that loads it, and a session ends when code is defined that is not Tempest's own (session). Revise evaluating an edited file, an @eval into a package and a function redefined at the prompt are one event to Julia, a world after the last, and end a session alike, so that the code a table's answer was derived from is the code the process runs. Tempest's own definitions, its kernels' functions, which every build makes, leave it standing (own_definition).\n\nAn answer missing is found outside the lock and kept with get!(table, key, answer, asked), asked the session read as the question was asked (session), the first kept winning where two tasks found one at once: the same answer found twice. An answer is its session's: one found while the session ended, by a definition on another thread, is of the code before and is returned to its caller but not kept. get!(f, table, key) does all three, finding it by f(); a caller whose f would be a closure over a value of its own type each time, a kernel's function say, reads the session, looks up with get and keeps with get!, so as not to compile a closure for each. Where finding an answer twice is more than waste, as a kernel's compilation is, making it once is the table's own business: its value is what makes it, created under the lock (cached_kernel).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.SessionTable"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.session","page":"Building kernels","text":"session() -> UInt\n\nThe session whose answers hold now: the one before, unless code was defined since that is not Tempest's own, which begins another. Julia counts a world for every definition, of a method, a type or a constant, by whatever made it; the world counter is read once per question. A question asked while one of Tempest's definitions makes its worlds, some microseconds, waits for that one to end, which counts them (own_definition), and for no other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.session"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.definitions","page":"Building kernels","text":"definitions(code) -> Vector{String}\n\nWhat a session table's key holds of code, functions, folds and types an answer was derived from, beside the objects themselves (see SessionTable): the content key of each no package identifies (see content_key), which writes it by its definition, as a store's key does, so that a function redefined in the session, the same object, is another key. A package's code is named by the object alone, since no code changes within a session (session). Sorted, so that code named in any order is named alike.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.definitions"},{"category":"function","location":"kernels/#Tempest.Compiler.in_own_store","page":"Building kernels","text":"in_own_store(f)\n\nf() with what it measures and decides kept in a store of its own, a new directory removed after: a package's precompile workload prepares in one, so that what its image compiles follows from its programs alone, never from what the store of the environment it ran in holds, and its preparations keep their decisions and a preparation of a program again replays them, as a session's do. The store is that of f and of the tasks it starts, a scoped value, not the process's: the environment's TEMPEST_CACHE is left as it is, and code running beside f keeps the store it had. What f starts is to end before f returns, since the store is removed then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tempest.Compiler.in_own_store"}]
}
